 to prioritize um  research focusing on individuals who  maybe are  are at greatest need and um so that you  don't have like all the trials  focusing on maybe the 13 of of  individuals who have  a mutation that's amenable to exxon 51  skipping for example  um and so i had a summer student been  high  last year who looked at clinical  trials.gov which is a repository  of of the repository of all clinical  trials basically every clinical trial at  least in the u.s has to be  pre-registered  and it's all the files are xml files and  so she downloaded all the xml files that  were related to duchenne muscular  dystrophy  and the great thing about r of course is  that they're all these packages that  allow you to interact with different  kinds of files  so even though you know usually i think  about using like  tab delimited or comma delimited files  you know even if they're xml files you  can still  use the xml package and you can actually  pick out some of um  some of the the blocks that kind of the  predefined blocks and then of course  perform data cleaning that includes  things like  redundant drug names every drug i think  has at least like four names that's a  whole sort of issue onto itself  um and then she used our shiny to design  and create a web interface to explore  these trials and actually look at you  can search by condition then you can get  you can get some summary statistics and  so on and again if you if you go to my  twitter account to look at look at the  the pinned tweet you can get to the  slides and you can  you can if you want to play around with  this and take a look at it  okay so uh the second the second  vignette that i wanted to talk about was  using molecular data sets  to try to try to help out with actually  finding biomarkers of either disease  progression or drug response  so i mentioned that there's a focus in  doing this especially for  for rare diseases and we focus on  looking for  um for metabolic biomarkers so basically  metabolites which are small molecules  the study of metabolites is called  metabolomics uh they can be detected in  various  uh biofluids and tissues that includes  things like blood urine  um and so forth and they have various uh  functions and and the good thing about  them is they can be influenced by both  genetics and environment so they're sort  of like sit somewhere in between  so it's good to have you know this  opportunity to look for for different  kinds of markers and i'll be  i'll be focusing on this first paper  that we worked on from 2016.  so it this paper looked at 51 dmd  patients and 22 healthy controls  uh we detected over 2000 metabolites the  samples were all run at georgetown  in terms of the metabolomics but they  are from five different study sites so  one of the things that we were always  very  we were careful to assess is to see  whether there's any possible batch  effects and  you can see actually the individuals are  not balanced between the study sites so  we did also have  um also a sub analysis just looking at  uh calgary albert  alberta children's hospital which did  actually have a more balanced number of  patients and  healthy controls so the first step i  just i always like to  to point out the first step in doing any  sort of oh my first of all doing a lot  of data exploration making sure that  everything sort of matches up  and then i guess the second step is to  do principal component analysis  because i think that it's very revealing  in terms of you know are there batch  effects  are there artifacts when people see for  example two clusters sometimes they get  very excited like i've discovered a  pattern in my data  for me it always just makes me feel  nervous because i worry like oh doesn't  mean that actually the samples are  processed differently so  we were actually pretty happy to see  this it's just color coded by age and  this color code by study site  uh now if i was to read you right now i  would redo it a little bit differently  i just wanted to kind of have this like  as a parenthesis because i feel like i  have learned something since 2016.  so i do try to use a colorblind friendly  palette because  i'm not color blind myself so it's not  something that comes automatically but i  think about four percent of the  population is colorblind  um but so there's one of these questions  like you know how can we kind of better  automate these things so you don't have  to make these decisions right which is  kind of like tiring and like i always  you know i was like i'm like going back  and changing the color blind  friendly palette i'm trying to get to  get better at this um and then for pca  now  because it is if it's all omics so  unless you're using  different types of measurements with  different units the recommendation is  generally to actually not scale so to  use the default for example if you're  doing uh print comp  in r and also to use the same distances  on both axes  so you can see like here between zero  this is zero and ten on the y  axis zero and ten on the x axis um  because  the first principal component has more  variability than the second principal  component so it's actually ideal to try  to maintain that as opposed to having it  be a square  and i learned a lot of a lot of these  tips from this this great anguian and  homes paper  which is also a great example of  reproducibility because they actually  have the code for generating all of the  figures in their paper  so i just trying to just share that okay  so in our study so basically we had  um you know over 2000 metabolites we  basically fit a regression model for  each of them  that had at the outcome a transformed  metabolite value  log transform various other quantile  normalized and so on  um and then we we looked at how that can  be expected value can be explained by  age study site and then um disease  status of course and then also age by  disease interaction  um and we found 14 metabolites that were  significantly altered  between dmd and healthy control that a  false discovery rate of one percent  out of those 14 because of the  technology that was used we didn't know  exactly what they were we just had an  idea  of what their weight was and some other  characteristics so um  the lab that we worked with the core at  georgetown did validate them using msms  and  uh they found um four of them creatine  creatinine  uh 5a dht and testosterone sulfate we're  pretty sure that you know this is  actually what they are  and l-arginine was was likely validated  um and this is actually  it doesn't seem like a great deal but  it's actually pretty common for these  kinds of experiments it's sort of like  part of the course like most things  actually don't get validated  uh creature and creation we were happy  to see because we know that they're  actually involved  in the creatine pathway which has to do  with with things that happen in muscle  and in blood  and you see creatine and creatinine here  so these are both metabolized they're  small molecules  and this reaction here is catalyzed by  creatine kinase protein kinase is a  protein so we didn't pick it up in our  study because it's too large  but actually our team did do some  members of our team could actually do a  proteomics study where they picked up  creatine kinase  and this is actually a very um a very  well-known biomarker  uh a very well-known diagnostic  biomarker of dmd so it shows up being at  very high levels  in sort of children between the ages of  maybe like four and ten  or so um because it shows that there's a  lot of a lot of like muscle  muscle damage going on so typically if  somebody is suspected of having dmd  they'll have a blood test or they look  at ck levels and typically those will be  way way higher than in  you know healthy healthy individuals but  the problem is that would age  as you know there's muscle wasting going  on and so would age that actually  goes down a lot and so you can't really  use it as a biomarker of disease  progression or  therapy response but in any case it was  good it was good to see  to see these as some of our some of our  among our results  and we also considered in a post-hoc  fashion the ratio between  creatine and creatinine because you see  that like one of them leads to the other  so they're actually inversely  related so their ratio is actually even  more um  it's actually even more compelling to  look at it so you can see between  dmd and controls they're they're  generally very very different  and it has a very low p-value but i mean  this was this was a kind of a post-hoc  analysis  uh but we were actually happy to see  this result validated by by another  group so that was that was good  um another thing i just i want to talk  about so in terms of doing this work i  think it's important to keep in mind  that there is kind of this i call it  like a study  ecosystem like what's going on beyond  the paper and you can think of this as  sort of  being related to reproducibility you  know reproducibility powered by r i  think that's one of the big benefits  of r so i know some people have have  said that a scientific article or a  report  it's sort of like advertising the  research because you're only kind of  getting  maybe like a big picture of you finding  out you know about like specific  decisions that were made  maybe in order to to have um to have  a certain result or a certain  publication but there's a lot that's  actually  kind of going on behind the scenes um  this is published by  in place and so plot always wanted to  deposit the data you know  as much as possible and so we deposited  the raw data and triad which is one of  the repositories that they recommend  and then the process data along with the  analysis code is on github  and they're all linked and ideally you  know you can you can get all the links  so they're all linked for example from  my website  i think it would be great to to figure  out some some better ways in the  community to sort of connect all these  things i mean there's also like links  you know in the in the paper but um it's  one of those things where again i think  if you kind of incentivize it and sort  of make it be automatic so that people  don't have to kind of think about  doing all of these things and kind of  figure out you know like every time you  know like i update my cv and it's like  you have to like kind of copy and paste  like five different links i think it  would be great to have  a more a more automated way of doing  that just like it would be great to have  maybe like you know i color blind  friendly palettes would just be the  default so you don't have to think  through it and make you know make more  decisions about it yourself  um so just some of the conclusions so  primarily for the for the metabolic  study so the 2016 study that i talked  about  we're very proud that it was the first  comprehensive metabolomic study for  producing muscular dystrophy and we  thought it was one of the first steps in  finding  metabolic surrogate biomarkers and  adding to  this list because people have looked at  other kinds of of uh you know like  proteomics and looking at  mrna markers and so on and we also had a  2020 study which looked at  the urine of mice so there's a mouse  model called the mdx model and we  compared  um healthy mice with mdx mice and then  um  they either were just taking a control  or they were on prednisolone which is a  type of corticosteroid so we got some  additional biomarkers from that as well  so some future goals in terms of also uh  combining this  with um with what i talked about in  terms of clinical trials i think it  would be really great to have these  types of studies embedded into clinical  trials because  clinical trials have very clean data  they have very good  clinical outcomes and so then it's a lot  i think it's a lot easier if you can  just have access to some of these  samples  where people have already you know the  the people who set up the clinical  trials are already measuring a lot of  these  a lot of these outcomes on the study  participants and then you can also do  kind of some omics sort of like on the  side i mean it has to be embedded into  the protocol but it now would be sort of  like  the ideal thing to do and i also tried  to connect this type of study with  genetic data and focus it on some of the  you know i mentioned the areas of  greatest need in terms of maybe looking  at people who  aren't served by current trials in some  way right either because they have a  rare mutation or because of their age or  because of some other  some other um aspects  okay so just this is just that i just  wanted to acknowledge all the people who  are involved in the study our funding  the study participants  um including these are three students  who who helped me out they weren't  involved in the original publications  because they started working  with me on this afterwards but uh bin  was involved in the  um in the clinical trials work and then  uh boyun and chintang are looking more  at the  metabolomics data we also did some  metabolomics proteomics integration  again if anybody's interested you know  definitely feel free to contact me  afterwards if anybody wants to talk more  about it  and just this is just my final  advertisement slide so we have  a master's program in health informatics  and data science at georgetown  um it's a great program if i say so  myself i'm one of the faculty in it  um we're in our second year right now so  we're open for applications for next  year definitely let me know  it also includes it's a one year program  that includes um a summer  sort of internship with industry which  is co-mentored by by a faculty member as  well  and um you know i i recently joined our  ladies dc as a co-organizer and i know  that our ladies is also involved in  setting up this meeting so  these are some of the twitter accounts  for our ladies 